IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- State Pension Credit Act 2002 (2002 c. 16) -- LAYER 1, the nouns.
--
-- Source: ../../registers/source-bundle/ukpga-2002-16.txt, a plain-text rendering made by a converter
-- script from legislation.gov.uk, point-in-time 2026-07-01. It is not the official
-- text. Every rule in the modules that import this one cites it with an @ref whose
-- src: locator is checked by srcrefs.py.
--
-- This module holds the nouns every other module shares: the people, the
-- facts a witness could state about them, and the amounts the Act leaves to
-- regulations. It holds no rule of the Act. One local date constructor, YMD, is
-- defined here because the stable l4 (1.6.98) does not have one.
--
-- UNITS. The Act never names a currency or a period for any amount it mentions.
-- Every NUMBER below that stands for money is in the one currency and the one
-- period (the regulations use a week) that the person supplying the figures uses
-- throughout; the Act itself fixes neither.

§ `State Pension Credit Act 2002 -- nouns`

-- The stable l4 has no YMD. Day first, as `Date` takes it.
GIVEN y IS A NUMBER
      m IS A NUMBER
      d IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
YearMonthDay y m d MEANS Date d m y -- renamed from YMD so it cannot collide with the YMD in newer daydate libraries; see docs/ENCODING-GUIDE.md section 5

§§ `People and their sex (section 1(6))`

-- Section 1(6) distinguishes a woman from a man. Nothing else in the Act does.
DECLARE Sex IS ONE OF
    Woman
    Man

-- One natural person: the claimant, or the other member of a couple.
--
-- "Pensionable age" is not defined by this Act. Section 17(1) hands it to the
-- rules in paragraph 1 of Schedule 4 to the Pensions Act 1995, which is not among
-- the inputs. So the two days at which pensionable age is attained are BORROWED
-- FACTS here, never worked out from a date of birth:
--   * `day pensionable age is attained`: the day this person attains pensionable
--     age as that Schedule defines it for this person (a woman's own; for a man, the
--     Schedule's own rule for men), as section 3(1)(a) and section 3ZA(3) use it;
--   * `day a woman born on the same day attains pensionable age`: the day a
--     woman born on this person's date of birth attains pensionable age, as
--     section 1(6)(b) uses it. For a woman, this is the same day as the first.
DECLARE Person HAS
    sex                                                       IS A Sex
    `date of birth`                                           IS A DATE
    `day pensionable age is attained`                         IS A DATE
    `day a woman born on the same day attains pensionable age` IS A DATE

§§ `Income (section 15(1))`

-- One amount for each of the ten descriptions of income in section 15(1), in
-- the text's own order. The Act leaves the way income is calculated or estimated
-- to regulations (section 15(3) to (6)), so each amount is the figure AS THE
-- PRESCRIBED RULES WORK IT OUT: the person states it, the Act does not compute it.
-- A description in which the person has no income is 0.
DECLARE Income HAS
    earnings                                                                        IS A NUMBER  -- (a)
    `working tax credit`                                                            IS A NUMBER  -- (b)
    `retirement pension income`                                                     IS A NUMBER  -- (c)
    `income from annuity contracts other than retirement pension income`            IS A NUMBER  -- (d)
    `prescribed social security benefits other than retirement pension income and state pension credit` IS A NUMBER  -- (e)
    `foreign social security benefits of a prescribed description`                  IS A NUMBER  -- (f)
    `a war disablement pension or war widow's or widower's pension`                 IS A NUMBER  -- (g)
    `a foreign war disablement pension or foreign war widow's or widower's pension` IS A NUMBER  -- (h)
    `income from capital`                                                           IS A NUMBER  -- (i)
    `income of a prescribed description`                                            IS A NUMBER  -- (j)

§§ `Couples (section 17(1) "couple")`

-- The four facts the definition of "couple" turns on. "Members of the same
-- household" is for regulations to say (section 17(2)(a)), so it is asked as a
-- fact, counting any treatment those regulations give.
DECLARE Household HAS
    `married to or civil partners of each other`                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `members of the same household`                                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `living together as a married couple or as civil partners`         IS A BOOLEAN
    `in prescribed circumstances that take them outside paragraph (b)` IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `The amounts the Act leaves to regulations (sections 2, 3 and 4)`

-- Everything the Act says "shall be a prescribed amount", "a prescribed percentage"
-- or "such amount as may be prescribed", and that is not among the inputs, is a
-- plain input here. These are NOT the Act's figures; the Act states no figure for
-- any of them. The tests use invented round numbers and say so.
--
-- A prescribed percentage is a number of per cent: 60 stands for 60 per cent. A
-- prescribed amount is in the one currency and the one period the person uses.
DECLARE Prescribed HAS
    `standard minimum guarantee for a member of a couple`                         IS A NUMBER        -- s 2(4), (5)(a)
    `standard minimum guarantee for a claimant who is not a member of a couple`   IS A NUMBER        -- s 2(5)(b): lower than the first
    `additional amount for a claimant who is severely disabled`                   IS A NUMBER        -- s 2(7)
    `additional amount for an allowance under section 70 or carer support payment` IS A NUMBER       -- s 2(8)
    `other prescribed additional amounts that are applicable`                      IS A NUMBER        -- s 2(3)(b)
    `prescribed amount substituted for the standard minimum guarantee`            IS A MAYBE NUMBER  -- s 2(6): JUST in a prescribed case
    `savings credit threshold`                                                    IS A NUMBER        -- s 3(7)
    `prescribed percentage for amount A`                                          IS A NUMBER        -- s 3(4), amount A (b)
    `prescribed percentage for amount B`                                          IS A NUMBER        -- s 3(4), amount B (a)
    `prescribed percentage for the maximum savings credit`                        IS A NUMBER        -- s 3(7)
    `prescribed higher amount substituted for the appropriate minimum guarantee in amount B` IS A MAYBE NUMBER  -- s 3(5)
    `prescribed amount below which state pension credit is not payable`           IS A NUMBER        -- s 4(3)

§§ `What a rule can answer`

-- Three answers, for a yes-or-no question the Act may not be able to settle.
-- "Not covered by this encoding" is the answer where the text itself hands the
-- case to regulations or to a pilot scheme whose content is not among the inputs
-- (sections 12 and 18A), so the Act's own rules cannot be said to decide it.
DECLARE Answer IS ONE OF
    `The Act says yes`
    `The Act says no`
    `Not covered by this encoding`

§§ `One claimant on one day (the case)`

-- The text's natural unit: one claimant, on one day, with the other member of a
-- couple if there is one. Every date-dependent rule is tested on `day tested`,
-- because the Act speaks in the present tense ("is entitled") and fixes no
-- test date of its own.
--
-- Where the text hands a judgment to someone else (regulations, the Secretary of
-- State, another Act) the judgment is a BOOLEAN here, worded as the Act words it,
-- and is never decided by the encoding.
DECLARE Case HAS
    `day tested`                          IS A DATE
    claimant                              IS A Person
    `other member of the couple`          IS A Person   -- read only where the claimant is a member of a couple
    household                             IS A Household
    `in Great Britain`                    IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 1(2)(a), counting any treatment under regulations (s 1(5)(a))
    `temporarily absent from Great Britain and entitlement continued by regulations` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 1(5)(b)
    `income of the claimant`              IS AN Income
    `qualifying income of the claimant`   IS A NUMBER   -- s 3(6): regulations say which income counts
    `income of the other member`          IS AN Income
    `qualifying income of the other member` IS A NUMBER
    `prescribed circumstances in which the income of the other member is not aggregated` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 5
    `severely disabled`                   IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 17(2)(b): as regulations treat the claimant
    `claimant entitled to an allowance under section 70 of the Contributions and Benefits Act or to carer support payment` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 2(8)(a)
    `other member entitled to an allowance under section 70 of the Contributions and Benefits Act or to carer support payment` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 2(8)(b)
    `other member entitled to state pension credit`  IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 4(1)
    `regulations under section 3ZA(1) prescribe the claimant's case` IS A BOOLEAN
    `regulations under section 3(8) take the maximum savings credit to be nil` IS A BOOLEAN
    `excluded by section 115 of the Immigration and Asylum Act 1999 as amended by section 4(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
    `prescribed circumstances in which an amount below the prescribed amount is payable` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 4(3)
    `a claim has been made and the Administration Act section 1 conditions are met` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 17(1) "entitled"
    `entitlement restricted by section 27 of the Social Security Act 1998`           IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 17(1) "entitled"
    `husband or wife by a marriage entered into under a law which permits polygamy`  IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 12(1)(a)
    `either party to the marriage has a spouse additional to the other party`        IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 12(1)(b)
    `person, other party and additional spouse members of the same household`        IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 12(1)(c)
    `a pilot scheme under section 18A applies`                                       IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 18A
